    "captions": [
      {
        "t": 0,
        "text": "2:30 p.m., 3 July 1863. Since about 1 p.m., some 150 Confederate guns have pounded Cemetery Ridge, and about 100 Union guns have answered. The ground shakes for miles."
      },
      {
        "t": 12,
        "text": "Henry Hunt, Meade's artillery chief, slows the Union fire to save ammunition. Across the valley, Lee's gunners think they have silenced the Union batteries."
      },
      {
        "t": 22,
        "text": "Longstreet, who has argued all day against the attack, cannot bring himself to give the order. When Pickett asks if he should advance, Longstreet only nods."
      },
      {
        "t": 30,
        "text": "About 3 p.m. Nine brigades, some 12,500 men, step out of the woods in lines a mile wide: Pettigrew and Trimble on the left, Pickett's Virginians on the right. Their aim is a small copse of trees in the Union centre."
      },
      {
        "t": 42,
        "text": "Union guns open on them from Cemetery Hill to Little Round Top. Shells tear gaps in the lines; the men close up and march on."
      },
      {
        "t": 52,
        "text": "On the far left, the 8th Ohio and the guns on Cemetery Hill fire into Pettigrew's flank. Brockenbrough's and Davis's brigades break first."
      },
      {
        "t": 58,
        "text": "Pickett's men wheel left to close on the copse. At the Emmitsburg Road they must climb stout rail fences under rifle fire, then canister: tins of iron balls that turn cannon into giant shotguns."
      },
      {
        "t": 66,
        "text": "Stannard's Vermont brigade swings out from the Union line and fires into Kemper's flank. Wilcox and Lang, sent to cover Pickett's right, come too late and are driven back by McGilvery's guns."
      },
      {
        "t": 72,
        "text": "At the Angle, General Lewis Armistead, his hat on his sword, leads a few hundred men over the stone wall. Lieutenant Alonzo Cushing, twice wounded, fires his last gun and is killed."
      },
      {
        "t": 76,
        "text": "Union regiments rush in from both sides. Armistead falls mortally wounded among Cushing's guns. Those inside the wall are killed or captured."
      },
      {
        "t": 82,
        "text": "It is over in less than an hour. Survivors stream back across the fields. About half the attackers have been killed, wounded or captured; Pickett's division has lost all three brigadiers."
      },
      {
        "t": 88,
        "text": "Lee rides out to meet them and tells them it is all his fault. When he tells Pickett to rally his division, Pickett is said to have answered that he has no division."
      },
      {
        "t": 95,
        "text": "Meade does not counter-attack. On 4 July, as Vicksburg surrenders, Lee begins his retreat to Virginia in the rain."
      }
    ]
  },
