    "captions": [
      {
        "t": 0,
        "text": "15 November 1864. Sherman's engineers burn Atlanta's depots, workshops and rail yards, and the fire spreads to much of the town. The next morning about 62,000 men march out to the east and south, singing 'John Brown's Body'."
      },
      {
        "t": 2,
        "text": "The army moves in two wings on four roads, to keep the Confederates guessing between Macon and Augusta. It carries twenty days' rations and plans to live off the land."
      },
      {
        "t": 4,
        "text": "Foragers, nicknamed 'bummers', strip farms of corn, hogs and horses. Railways are torn up, the rails heated over bonfires and twisted round trees into 'Sherman's neckties'."
      },
      {
        "t": 7,
        "text": "22 November. At Griswoldville, Georgia militia, many of them old men and boys, attack a Union brigade dug in behind fence rails and are cut down."
      },
      {
        "t": 8,
        "text": "The Left Wing marches into Milledgeville, the state capital. The legislature has fled. Officers hold a mock session in the empty statehouse and vote Georgia back into the Union."
      },
      {
        "t": 12,
        "text": "Thousands of enslaved people leave the plantations and follow the columns. Sherman, worried about feeding them, tries to turn them back."
      },
      {
        "t": 18,
        "text": "At Millen the soldiers find Camp Lawton, a prison pen emptied days before. The sight of the holes the prisoners lived in hardens their mood."
      },
      {
        "t": 19,
        "text": "Kilpatrick's cavalry feints towards Augusta and fights Wheeler's horsemen at Waynesboro on 4 December."
      },
      {
        "t": 24,
        "text": "9 December. At Ebenezer Creek, a Union corps pulls up its pontoon bridge before the freedpeople following it can cross. With Wheeler's cavalry behind them, many drown trying to swim."
      },
      {
        "t": 25,
        "text": "The army reaches the flooded rice fields outside Savannah, where about 10,000 Confederates under William Hardee hold the causeways."
      },
      {
        "t": 28,
        "text": "13 December. Hazen's division storms Fort McAllister in fifteen minutes, opening the Ogeechee River. Sherman is back in touch with the navy and its supply ships."
      },
      {
        "t": 35,
        "text": "On the night of 20 December, Hardee slips his army out across a pontoon bridge into South Carolina. The mayor surrenders the city the next morning."
      },
      {
        "t": 36,
        "text": "22 December. Sherman telegraphs Lincoln: 'I beg to present you as a Christmas gift the city of Savannah.'"
      }
    ]
